s($,"ke0","idK",()=>{var q=null
return A.a([A.dX("The keyboard","Piano keys repeat in a pattern: white keys, with black keys in groups of two and three. The white key just left of a group of two black keys is C. Every C sounds the same note, higher or lower. Today you play C, D and E with the right hand.",new A.h5x(),q),A.dX("Middle C","Middle C is the C nearest the middle of the keyboard. On the music it sits on its own short line just below the top staff. Put your right thumb on it. Play middle C and its neighbour D.",new A.h5y(),q),A.dX("Finger numbers","Fingers are numbered 1 to 5 on each hand: thumb 1, then index, middle, ring and little finger 5. The small numbers above the notes say which finger to use. The keyboard under the music lights each key with its number. Play C D E F G with fingers 1 2 3 4 5.",new A.h5z(),q),A.dX("The staff","Music is written on five lines called the staff. Each note sits on a line or in a space; higher on the staff means higher in sound. From a line to the next space is one key up. Watch the notes climb and fall as you play.",new A.h5K(),q),A.dX("Treble clef","The curly sign at the start of the top staff is the treble clef, also called the G clef. Its curl wraps the line for the G above middle C. The right hand reads this staff. Today the tune moves around G.",new A.h5V(),q),A.dX("Right-hand C position","Put your right thumb on middle C and rest each finger on the next white key: C D E F G. This is the C position. Keep the fingers curved and stay on your five keys. The drill uses all five fingers.",new A.h65(),q),A.dX("Quarter, half and whole notes","A quarter note has a filled head and a stem and lasts one beat. A half note has an open head and a stem and lasts two beats. A whole note has an open head and no stem and lasts four. Count the beats out loud while you play.",new A.h6g(),q),A.dX("Four beats and barlines","The 4 over 4 at the start means four beats in every bar. Barlines split the music into bars of the same length. The first beat of each bar is the strongest; the count-in ticks it a little higher. Count 1 2 3 4 in every bar.",new A.h6r(),q),A.dX("A steady beat","A steady beat matters more than speed. Start at a speed where you never have to stop. Turn on Count-in for one bar of ticks before the music starts. Keep the same pulse all the way round the loop.",new A.h6C(),q),A.dX("Your first phrase","Ode to Joy, by Beethoven, opens with notes you already know from the C position. The right hand starts on E with finger 3. Play bars 1 to 4 with the right hand only; the left hand stays silent. This is your first real tune.",new A.h6N(),q),A.dX("Bass clef","The sign with two dots on the bottom staff is the bass clef, also called the F clef. Its two dots sit either side of the line for the F below middle C. The left hand reads this staff. Notes on it sound lower than the top staff.",new A.h6Y(),q),A.dX("Left-hand C position","Put your left little finger on the C below middle C and your thumb on G. The fingers on C D E F G are 5 4 3 2 1, the other way round from the right hand. Keep the hand still and let the fingers do the work.",new A.h5A(),q),A.dX("Reading two staves","The two staves joined by a brace are called the grand staff. The top staff is for the right hand and the bottom staff for the left. Notes lined up above each other are played at the same moment. Today the hands take turns, one bar each.",new A.h5B(),q),A.dX("Hands together","Now both hands play at once, moving in the same direction. The right thumb is on middle C and the left thumb on G, so the fingers do not match: watch the numbers. Go very slowly; it feels strange at first, and that is normal.",new A.h5C(),q),A.dX("Rests","A rest is a silence with a length, just like a note. A quarter rest lasts one beat, a half rest two, and a whole rest a full bar. Keep counting through the rests so the next note comes in on time.",new A.h5D(),q),A.dX("Dotted half note","A dot after a note adds half its length again. A dotted half note lasts three beats: two plus one. Hold it and count all three beats before you move on.",new A.h5E(),q),A.dX("Three beats in a bar","With 3 over 4 at the start there are three beats in every bar, as in a waltz. The first beat is the strong one: ONE two three. A dotted half note fills a whole bar.",new A.h5F(),q),A.dX("Tune and accompaniment","Often the right hand plays the tune while the left hand holds long notes underneath. Keep the left hand quiet and steady. Today the left hand holds one note per bar.",new A.h5G(),q),A.dX("Ode to Joy, bars 5 to 8","Bars 5 to 8 repeat the opening, but end on C instead of D. Play them with the right hand. Notice the dotted rhythm near the end of bar 8.",new A.h5H(),q),A.dX("Ode to Joy, hands together","Now play bars 1 to 8 with both hands. The left hand holds two notes for each chord, so it moves only a few times. Keep the slow speed until it flows.",new A.h5I(),q),A.dX("Eighth notes",'An eighth note lasts half a beat, so two of them fill one beat. Eighth notes have a flag, or are joined in pairs by a beam. Count them "1 and 2 and 3 and 4 and".',new A.h5J(),q),A.dX('Counting "1 and"','Say "and" halfway between the beats: 1 and 2 and. Notes on the numbers fall on the beat; notes on "and" fall between. Tap your foot on the numbers only.',new A.h5L(),q),A.dX("Dotted quarter and eighth",'A dotted quarter lasts one and a half beats, and the eighth after it fills the rest of the second beat. Count "1 (and) 2 AND". Ode to Joy uses this rhythm at the end of a line.',new A.h5M(),q),A.dX("Ties","A tie is a curved line joining two notes of the same pitch. Play the first note and hold it through the second without playing again. A tie lets a note last across a barline.",new A.h5N(),q),A.dX("Tempo","Tempo is the speed of the beat, counted in beats per minute: 60 means one beat every second. Tap the speed readout to set a goal tempo. Practise below it and move up a little at a time.",new A.h5O(),q),A.dX("Slowing down","Slow practice is not a step backwards. The speed control slows the music without changing its pitch. If you stumble, take 10 % off and play it again.",new A.h5P(),q),A.dX("How to drill a loop","Pick the bars that trip you up and loop only those. Play the loop several times in a row without stopping. When it feels easy, widen the loop by one bar on each side.",new A.h5Q(),q),A.dX("Ode to Joy, the middle","The middle of Ode to Joy starts on D and uses pairs of eighth notes. Loop bars 9 to 12 with both hands. Keep the eighths even.",new A.h5R(),q),A.dX("Ode to Joy, the end","The last four bars bring back the opening tune. Play bars 9 to 16 with both hands. Keep the speed steady across the join.",new A.h5S(),q),A.dX("Ode to Joy, complete","Today you play the whole tune with both hands, all sixteen bars. Start slowly and keep going even if a note slips. Use the speed control to find a pace you can hold.",new A.h5T(),q),A.dX("Whole and half steps","A half step goes to the very next key, white or black. A whole step is two half steps. E to F and B to C are half steps with no black key between them.",new A.h5U(),q),A.dX("The major scale","A major scale is eight notes in the pattern whole, whole, half, whole, whole, whole, half. Started on C, it uses only white keys. It runs from C up to the next C and back.",new A.h5W(),q),A.dX("C major, right hand","Right-hand fingering for C major is 1 2 3, then the thumb on F, then 2 3 4 5. Coming down, finger 3 crosses over the thumb onto E. The numbers on the keys show each finger.",new A.h5X(),q),A.dX("C major, left hand","Left-hand fingering for C major is 5 4 3 2 1, then finger 3 crosses over onto A, then 2 1. Coming down, the thumb passes under after G. Go slowly where the hand turns.",new A.h5Y(),q),A.dX("Thumb under","In the right hand going up, the thumb slides under the hand after finger 3 to land on F. Move the thumb early, while finger 3 is still playing. Keep the wrist level.",new A.h5Z(),new A.h6_()),A.dX("C major, hands together","Now both hands play the scale together. The hands turn at different places, so play very slowly and watch both sets of numbers. Stop the loop at a speed where neither hand hesitates.",new A.h60(),q),A.dX("Two octaves","The built-in C major scale climbs two octaves, hands together. The right thumb turns under twice on the way up. Loop the first two bars until the turns are smooth.",new A.h61(),q),A.dX("G major and its sharp","G major has one sharp: F sharp, the black key just right of F. The key signature at the start marks it once for the whole piece. The fingering is the same as in C major.",new A.h62(),q),A.dX("F major and its flat","F major has one flat: B flat, the black key just left of B. Its right-hand fingering is not the same as in C, so no finger numbers are shown here. Play it slowly and find a fingering that keeps the thumb off the black key.",new A.h63(),q),A.dX("Sharps, flats and naturals","A sharp raises a note by a half step, a flat lowers it, and a natural cancels either. The key signature applies to every bar. D major has two sharps, F sharp and C sharp.",new A.h64(),q),A.dX("Intervals","An interval is the distance between two notes, counted in letter names: C to E is a third, C to G a fifth. Count both ends. Play each pair one after the other, then together.",new A.h66(),q),A.dX("Triads","A triad is three notes stacked in thirds: root, third and fifth. C E G is the C major triad. In the C position you play it with fingers 1 3 5.",new A.h67(),q),A.dX("The C, F and G chords","In C major the three main chords are C (C E G), F (F A C) and G (G B D). They are built on the first, fourth and fifth notes of the scale. Today the left hand plays them as blocks.",new A.h68(),q),A.dX("I \u2013 IV \u2013 V \u2013 I","Roman numerals name chords by scale step: in C major, I is C, IV is F and V is G. I\u2013IV\u2013V\u2013I is one of the most common chord patterns. Listen to how V pulls back home to I.",new A.h69(),q),A.dX("Broken chords","A broken chord plays the notes of a chord one after another instead of together. Root, third, fifth, third is a common pattern. The left hand plays it under the same chords as yesterday.",new A.h6a(),q),A.dX("C arpeggio","An arpeggio runs up and down the notes of one chord. C E G C uses only the notes of the C chord, then turns and comes back. Let the hand open a little wider than the five-finger position.",new A.h6b(),q),A.dX("Left-hand patterns","The left hand often keeps a pattern going, such as root, fifth, octave, fifth. A steady pattern frees the right hand for the tune. Keep the pattern the same in every bar while the chord changes.",new A.h6c(),q),A.dX("A chord under a melody","Now put chords under a simple tune. The left hand holds one chord per bar while the right hand plays the melody. Each chord shares a note with the tune above it.",new A.h6d(),q),A.dX("I \u2013 IV \u2013 V \u2013 I in G","The same pattern works in every key. In G major, I is G, IV is C and V is D. Notice the F sharp in the D chord.",new A.h6e(),q),A.dX("Chords in Ode to Joy","The left hand of the built-in Ode to Joy plays the root and fifth of the I and V chords. Play the whole tune and listen to how the chords change under the melody. Name each chord, I or V, as it comes.",new A.h6f(),q),A.dX("D major","D major has two sharps, F sharp and C sharp. The fingering is the same as in C and G major. Look at the key signature before you start.",new A.h6h(),q),A.dX("A major","A major has three sharps: F, C and G sharp. The right-hand fingering is again 1 2 3, thumb under, 1 2 3 4 5. Notice how the black keys shape the hand.",new A.h6i(),q),A.dX("B flat major","B flat major has two flats, B flat and E flat. Its fingering differs from the sharp keys, so no finger numbers are shown. Play slowly and keep the thumb on white keys.",new A.h6j(),q),A.dX("E flat major","E flat major has three flats: B, E and A flat. Like B flat, its fingering is not shown here. Look at each note before you play it.",new A.h6k(),q),A.dX("The circle of fifths","Each step up a fifth adds one sharp: C, G, D, A, E. Each step down a fifth adds one flat: F, B flat, E flat. That circle gives every key signature in order. Today: the D five-finger position.",new A.h6l(),q),A.dX("A minor","A minor uses the same white keys as C major but starts on A. It sounds darker because the half steps fall in different places. No finger numbers are shown for minor scales here.",new A.h6m(),q),A.dX("D minor","D minor has one flat, B flat, like F major. It starts and ends on D. Listen for the minor third, D to F.",new A.h6n(),q),A.dX("E minor","E minor has one sharp, F sharp, like G major. It starts and ends on E. Play it and compare it with G major.",new A.h6o(),q),A.dX("Relative keys","Every major key has a relative minor with the same key signature: C major and A minor, F major and D minor, G major and E minor. The minor key starts on the sixth note of the major scale. Today: the A minor five-finger position.",new A.h6p(),q),A.dX("G major, hands together","Review: the G major scale with both hands. Both hands use the same fingering as in C major. Keep the F sharp in mind going both ways.",new A.h6q(),q),A.dX("A finger pattern","This pattern, after Hanon's exercise No. 1 (1873), climbs one step each bar so every finger does the same work. Loop the first two bars. Keep every note equally strong.",new A.h6s(),q),A.dX("The pattern climbs","Now loop bars 1 to 4. The hand moves up one key each bar and the fingering stays the same. Let the wrist travel smoothly.",new A.h6t(),q),A.dX("Keep climbing","Bars 5 to 8 carry the pattern higher. Same fingers, new keys. Stay relaxed in the shoulders.",new A.h6u(),q),A.dX("Sixteenth notes",'A sixteenth note lasts a quarter of a beat; four fill one beat. Count them "1 e and a". The finger pattern is written in sixteenths.',new A.h6v(),q),A.dX("Evenness","Even playing means every note has the same length and weight. Play slowly and listen for any note that rushes or drops. The app cannot hear you yet, so this one is your ear's job.",new A.h6w(),q),A.dX("The pattern comes down","From bar 15 the pattern turns and comes down, starting from the top note. The fingering is mirrored. Loop bars 15 to 18.",new A.h6x(),q),A.dX("Pattern A","A new pattern in the C position: 1 3 2 4 3 5 4 2. Each finger stays on its own key, so the hand never moves. Play it with the right hand.",new A.h6y(),q),A.dX("Pattern A, both hands","The same pattern with both hands. The left hand plays the mirror: the same keys, so the finger numbers differ. Go slowly at first.",new A.h6z(),q),A.dX("Pattern B","Pattern B keeps the thumb coming back: 1 2 1 3 1 4 1 5. It trains the fingers to move while the thumb stays put. Keep the thumb light.",new A.h6A(),q),A.dX("The whole climb","Play the climbing half of the finger pattern, bars 1 to 14. It is long: keep a speed you can hold all the way. If one bar trips you, loop just that bar for a while.",new A.h6B(),q),A.dX("Twelve-bar form","The twelve-bar blues is twelve bars in a fixed chord order: four bars of I, two of IV, two of I, then V, IV, I, V. Count the bars as you play. Loop the first four bars of the built-in blues.",new A.h6D(),q),A.dX("The three chords","In C the blues uses the chords C, F and G: I, IV and V. Today the left hand walks root, fifth, sixth, fifth through the whole form. The right hand rests.",new A.h6E(),q),A.dX("The blues scale",'The blues scale in C is C, E flat, F, F sharp, G, B flat, C. Its "blue notes" lean the major scale toward minor. Play it up and down with the right hand.',new A.h6F(),q),A.dX("The left-hand shuffle","The shuffle rocks between root plus fifth and root plus sixth. In real blues the eighths are swung, long then short; the app plays them even, so swing them yourself once the notes are easy. Start with the first four bars.",new A.h6G(),q),A.dX("Right-hand riffs","A riff is a short phrase repeated over the changing chords. Today the right hand plays one riff on the chord's third and fifth. The left hand rests.",new A.h6H(),q),A.dX("Blues hands together","Put the hands together on the built-in blues. Loop the first four bars, then try the whole form. Keep the left hand steady even if the right hand slips.",new A.h6I(),q),A.dX("Blues in F","The same form in F: the chords are F, B flat and C. Notice the B flat in the key signature. The pattern in both hands stays the same shape.",new A.h6J(),q),A.dX("Blues in G","And in G: the chords are G, C and D. The F sharp shows up in the D chord. Same shapes, new keys.",new A.h6K(),q),A.dX("The shuffle all the way","Play the shuffle through all twelve bars with the left hand. Keep counting the bars so the chord changes come on time. The pattern moves with each chord root.",new A.h6L(),q),A.dX("Blues review","Review: the built-in twelve-bar blues in C with both hands, start to finish. Count the form as you go. Keep going through any slips.",new A.h6M(),q),A.dX("Ledger lines above","Notes above the staff get short extra lines called ledger lines. Count up from the top line: line, space, line. Today the right hand plays high A, B and C.",new A.h6O(),q),A.dX("Ledger lines below","Below the bass staff, ledger lines work the same way, counting down. Middle C sits on one ledger line: below the top staff, or above the bottom one. Today the left hand plays low notes.",new A.h6P(),q),A.dX("Reading by interval","Instead of naming every note, read the jump. From a line to the next space is a step; from a line to the next line is a skip. A step moves to the next finger; a skip leaves one out.",new A.h6Q(),q),A.dX("Key signatures again","The sharps or flats right after the clef are the key signature. They apply to every bar unless an accidental changes a note. This drill is in G: every F is F sharp.",new A.h6R(),q),A.dX("Time signatures again","The top number of the time signature counts the beats in a bar. The bottom number says which note gets one beat: 4 is a quarter note, 8 an eighth. This drill is in 2 over 4: two beats a bar.",new A.h6S(),q),A.dX("Six eighths in a bar","In 6 over 8 there are six eighth notes in a bar, felt as two big beats of three: ONE two three FOUR five six. With Count-in on, it ticks the two big beats. A dotted quarter fills one big beat.",new A.h6T(),q),A.dX("Noteheads and stems","A note has a head and, for most lengths, a stem. Stems point down on notes above the middle line and up on notes below it. Open heads are half and whole notes; filled heads are quarters and shorter. Look for each kind in the drill.",new A.h6U(),q),A.dX("Flags and beams","Eighth notes have one flag and sixteenth notes two. When they are grouped, the flags become beams joining the stems, usually one beat at a time. The drill has both eighths and sixteenths.",new A.h6V(),q),A.dX("Accidentals and dots","An accidental sits just before the notehead, on the same line or space. A dot sits just after the head. Writing your own notes in the app comes in a later version; for now, find these signs in the drill.",new A.h6W(),q),A.dX("A new tune to read","Read a tune you have not seen before, in G with both hands. Look at the key, the time and the first notes before you press Play. Then keep going and do not stop for slips.",new A.h6X(),q),A.dX("Review: C position","Unit 10 puts everything together at full speed. Start with the C five-finger position, both hands. Slow down if you need to, then build back up to 100 %.",new A.h6Z(),q),A.dX("Review: G position","The same at full speed in G: both hands, five fingers each. Keep the F sharp in mind. Build up to 100 % step by step.",new A.h7_(),q),A.dX("Review: F position","Now in F, with B flat. The five-finger position keeps each finger on one key. Full speed is the goal.",new A.h70(),q),A.dX("Ode to Joy at full speed","Play the whole of Ode to Joy at the written tempo. If it feels rushed, practise at 90 % first. The goal is the full sixteen bars.",new A.h71(),q),A.dX("The blues at full speed","The built-in twelve-bar blues, all twelve bars, at full speed. Count the form as you play. Keep the left hand steady.",new A.h72(),q),A.dX("Five-finger warm-up","The built-in five-finger warm-up, both hands, at full speed. It is a good way to start any practice. Keep each finger on its own key.",new A.h73(),q),A.dX("The scale at full speed","The built-in C major scale, two octaves, hands together, at full speed. Keep the thumb turns smooth. Slow down for a few rounds if a turn bumps.",new A.h74(),q),A.dX("The finger pattern at full speed","The climbing half of the finger pattern at full speed. Even notes matter more than fast ones. Keep the hand loose.",new A.h75(),q),A.dX("G major scale at full speed","The G major scale, hands together, at full speed. Watch the F sharp in both hands. One more day to go.",new A.h76(),q),A.dX("Your three best","Day 100. Pick your three best pieces from the Library and play each one start to finish at full speed. The drill here is Ode to Joy, all the way through. A hundred days of practice: well done.",new A.h77(),q)],A.ay("K<bvl>"))})
